rent_Approaches_and_New_Perspectives_in_Aegean_Iconography_AEGIS_2020_pp._223-252", "source_type": "paper", "published_at": null}]},
{"id": "20260715174340539079_7bea7ff9", "type": "Claim", "text": "The engraved scene on the Ring of Nestor (CMS VI, no. 277) is 
organized around a key pictorial unit that Arthur Evans described as a ‘watch-dog’ or ‘dragon’, which functions as the guardian of 
an old ‘tree of the world’ whose branches carry an exceptionally populous ritual scene in Aegean glyptic art.", "source_ids": 
["20260715174314431086_1e91a4c4"], "citations": [15], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260715174314431086_1e91a4c4", "title": "(2020) A.
G. Vlachopoulos, “The Ring of Nestor and the quest for authenticity”, in F. Blakolmer (ed.) Current Approaches and New Perspectives 
in Aegean Iconography, Aegis 18 (2020), pp. 223-252.", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/43416584/_2020_A.G._Vlachopoulos_The_Ring_of_Nestor_and_the_quest_for_authenticity_in_F._Blakolmer_ed._Cur
rent_Approaches_and_New_Perspectives_in_Aegean_Iconography_AEGIS_2020_pp._223-252", "source_type": "paper", "published_at": null}]},
{"id": "20260715174340567836_88ba374f", "type": "Claim", "text": "In recent scholarship, a prominent guardian motif on the Ring of 
Nestor’s engraved scene is re‑identified as a scorpion, interpreted as a major apotropaic symbol in Bronze Age Aegean iconography 
that ‘kills’ the so‑called Minoan dragon on the ring and thereby provides a coherent reading of the ring’s iconographic 
composition.", "source_ids": ["20260715174314431086_1e91a4c4"], "citations": [15], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260715174314431086_1e91a4c4", "title": "(2020) A. G. Vlachopoulos, “The Ring of Nestor and the quest for authenticity”, in F. 
Blakolmer (ed.) Current Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean Iconography, Aegis 18 (2020), pp. 223-252.", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/43416584/_2020_A.G._Vlachopoulos_The_Ring_of_Nestor_and_the_quest_for_authenticity_in_F._Blakolmer_ed._Cur
rent_Approaches_and_New_Perspectives_in_Aegean_Iconography_AEGIS_2020_pp._223-252", "source_type": "paper", "published_at": null}]},
{"id": "20260715174340594533_42dab730", "type": "Claim", "text": "The Ring of Nestor’s scene is interpreted as a detailed ritual 
sacrifice composition, with the overall iconography suggesting ceremonial activity, although the available descriptions do not 
enumerate specific participants, animals, or implements.", "source_ids": ["20260715174023619226_6420d547"], "citations": [11], 
"sources": [{"source_id": "20260715174023619226_6420d547", "title": "Rare 3,500-Year-Old Mycenaean Gold Signet Ring Discovered in 
Pylos | IttiHaas Chronicle", "url": 
"https://ittihaas.com/api/apps/685bf8c05cf93a74890518d0/functions/newsSharePage?newsId=69dbcfbed00190d9a09ce39a&slug=rare-3500-year-
old-mycenaean-gold-signet-ring-discovered-in-pylos", "source_type": "news", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": 
"20260715174340610572_9fee8d2c", "type": "Claim", "text": "Current iconographic studies place the Ring of Nestor’s imagery within 
broader Bronze Age Aegean glyptic traditions, emphasizing that recent finds with comparable iconographic subjects (including 
wall‑paintings, gold rings, figurines, and ivories) and technical study of signet‑ring craftsmanship have strengthened the view that
the ring’s complex scene belongs to an authentic Late Bronze Age (16th–15th century BC) visual repertoire rather than being a modern
forgery.", "source_ids": ["20260715174314431086_1e91a4c4"], "citations": [15], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260715174314431086_1e91a4c4", "title": "(2020) A. G. Vlachopoulos, “The Ring of Nestor and the quest for authenticity”, in F. 
Blakolmer (ed.) Current Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean Iconography, Aegis 18 (2020), pp. 223-252.", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/43416584/_2020_A.G._Vlachopoulos_The_Ring_of_Nestor_and_the_quest_for_authenticity_in_F._Blakolmer_ed._Cur
rent_Approaches_and_New_Perspectives_in_Aegean_Iconography_AEGIS_2020_pp._223-252", "source_type": "paper", "published_at": null}]},
{"id": "20260715174340641531_be889b6d", "type": "Claim", "text": "Some secondary interpretive summaries describe the Ring of 
Nestor’s engraved imagery as including a female deity (possibly associated with Demeter), grain, and an eagle of Zeus, and as 
symbolizing the four seasons along with sun and moon to express concepts of fertility and eternity; however, these elements are 
presented as speculative iconographic readings rather than firmly established identifications.", "source_ids": 
["20260715174314405964_9d852ceb"], "citations": [13], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260715174314405964_9d852ceb", "title": "ON THE 
FAMOUS MYCENAEAN SIGNET RING, CMS I, 179, OF THE TIRYNS TREASURE", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/4389733/ON_THE_FAMOUS_MYCENAEAN_SIGNET_RING_CMS_I_179_OF_THE_TIRYNS_TREASURE", "source_type": "paper", 
"published_at": null}]}], "offset": 0, "limit": 40, "total": 29, "next_offset": null}
