(metal jewellery and accoutrements) used by certain community members in Early and Middle Bronze Age Cyprus, would have served as 
tokens of self–representation and embodied collective identity that transcended gender and possibly age.”", "source_ids": 
["20260715175719070732_5fed65d8"], "citations": [21], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260715175719070732_5fed65d8", "title": "[PDF] A 
Gold Signet Ring (CMS I 16) of the Aegean Late Bronze Age in it", "url": 
"https://archaeopresspublishing.com/ojs/index.php/JGA/article/download/542/210/7244", "source_type": "paper", "published_at": 
null}]}, {"id": "20260715175720235144_102ec701", "type": "Evidence", "text": "“Chapin’s exploration of the fresco decoration of Room
31 in the Cult Centre at Mycenae breaks the anonymity of Aegean art as she attempts to unveil the identity of the three painted 
female figures. Differentiations in costume, attribute, posture and gesture set the canvas upon which elite identities, in this case
female ones, are detected in relation to Late Bronze Age Aegean ritual performance, hinting – in particular – at the practice of 
ruler cult at the site.”", "source_ids": ["20260715175719070732_5fed65d8"], "citations": [21], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260715175719070732_5fed65d8", "title": "[PDF] A Gold Signet Ring (CMS I 16) of the Aegean Late Bronze Age in it", "url": 
"https://archaeopresspublishing.com/ojs/index.php/JGA/article/download/542/210/7244", "source_type": "paper", "published_at": 
null}]}, {"id": "20260715175720249455_60a93cb6", "type": "Evidence", "text": "“Mikrakis discusses how LBA/EIA elites based their 
claims to authority on feasting practices and conspicuous consumption rather than warfare or the supernatural: ‘masculinity in 
Greece involved not only fully socialised aggression but also specific modes of song, dance and conviviality’ (p. xi).”", 
"source_ids": ["20260715175719070732_5fed65d8"], "citations": [21], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260715175719070732_5fed65d8", 
"title": "[PDF] A Gold Signet Ring (CMS I 16) of the Aegean Late Bronze Age in it", "url": 
"https://archaeopresspublishing.com/ojs/index.php/JGA/article/download/542/210/7244", "source_type": "paper", "published_at": 
null}]}, {"id": "20260715175720263387_f1416c77", "type": "Evidence", "text": "“Elefanti and Panagopoulou explore the manufacturing 
body and how the concept of complementary flaking techniques and individual actions ‘represent the result of social intelligence and
bodily executed tradition’ in the Middle Palaeolithic Lakonis Cave I (p. 117). Social acts (such as food preparation and 
consumption, tool making and knowledge transfer) directly associated with the cave’s large hearth complexes would have facilitated 
the expression of the embodied personal and social identities of its Neanderthal occupants.”", "source_ids": 
["20260715175719070732_5fed65d8"], "citations": [21], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260715175719070732_5fed65d8", "title": "[PDF] A 
Gold Signet Ring (CMS I 16) of the Aegean Late Bronze Age in it", "url": 
"https://archaeopresspublishing.com/ojs/index.php/JGA/article/download/542/210/7244", "source_type": "paper", "published_at": 
null}]}, {"id": "20260715175720277474_831e42af", "type": "Evidence", "text": "“Kopanias and Fox’s study of the cranium of a female 
adult individual, ‘Skeleton 2’, from Tell Nader in the Kurdistan region of Iraq points to the intentional modification of the 
cranium and to the practice’s link to group identity in the Ubaid; their work confirms Lorentz’s earlier work on circumferential 
