"citations": [21], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260715175719070732_5fed65d8", "title": "[PDF] A Gold Signet Ring (CMS I 16) of the 
Aegean Late Bronze Age in it", "url": "https://archaeopresspublishing.com/ojs/index.php/JGA/article/download/542/210/7244", 
"source_type": "paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260715175720291301_22855894", "type": "Evidence", "text": "“The 
importance of burial and (post)funerary ritual ‘as a means of constituting highly local identities’ (p. xi) and the complexity of 
Aegean deathways are topics addressed by a group of papers on Minoan Crete (Girella and Todaro; Leggara Herrero; Hatzaki) and Late 
Bronze Age and transitional Bronze Age/Iron Age mainland Greece (Galanakis; Papadimitriou; Whitley).”", "source_ids": 
["20260715175719070732_5fed65d8"], "citations": [21], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260715175719070732_5fed65d8", "title": "[PDF] A 
Gold Signet Ring (CMS I 16) of the Aegean Late Bronze Age in it", "url": 
"https://archaeopresspublishing.com/ojs/index.php/JGA/article/download/542/210/7244", "source_type": "paper", "published_at": 
null}]}, {"id": "20260715175720305596_bd1fb898", "type": "Evidence", "text": "“Does this edited volume achieve the goals set by the 
editors? Definitely yes. By breaking regional and chronological barriers, the editors have succeeded in bringing together 
twenty–nine contributions that cover a variety of perspectives on the perception, construction and performance of prehistoric 
identities and provide a useful overview of the complexity and plurality of these notions in the eastern Mediterranean. The 
theoretical and critical engagement with the terms ‘body’ and ‘embodied identity’ is persistent throughout the volume.”", 
"source_ids": ["20260715175719070732_5fed65d8"], "citations": [21], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260715175719070732_5fed65d8", 
"title": "[PDF] A Gold Signet Ring (CMS I 16) of the Aegean Late Bronze Age in it", "url": 
"https://archaeopresspublishing.com/ojs/index.php/JGA/article/download/542/210/7244", "source_type": "paper", "published_at": 
null}]}, {"id": "20260715175720319926_73970ef1", "type": "Evidence", "text": "“I could not agree more with John Robb’s statement 
that ‘this book represents a key moment in turning away from regionalism towards a broader outlook’ (p. vii), and with Kotsakis’ 
closing remark that ‘this turn to the individual bodies seems like a bold, yet very significant, step forward’ (p. 226).”", 
"source_ids": ["20260715175719070732_5fed65d8"], "citations": [21], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260715175719070732_5fed65d8", 
"title": "[PDF] A Gold Signet Ring (CMS I 16) of the Aegean Late Bronze Age in it", "url": 
"https://archaeopresspublishing.com/ojs/index.php/JGA/article/download/542/210/7244", "source_type": "paper", "published_at": 
null}]}, {"id": "20260715175720333517_f65a1113", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Pylos was a significant Mycenaean Bronze Age city 
located in the region of Messenia, Greece. The site is situated on the hill of Ano Englianos and during its Late Bronze Age 
occupation between c. 1600-1200 BCE it covered a maximum area of 200-300,000 square metres (20-30 hectares).", "source_ids": 
["20260715175719083632_2a1929df"], "citations": [22], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260715175719083632_2a1929df", "title": "The 
Archaeology of Bronze Age Mycenaean Pylos Brewminate: A Bold Blend of News and Ideas", "url": 
"https://brewminate.com/the-archaeology-of-bronze-age-mycenaean-pylos", "source_type": "blog", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": 
"20260715175720347382_904f6454", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Pylos has provided the largest collection of Linear B tablets on the 
mainland and is the best-preserved palatial centre of the Mycenaean Civilization.", "source_ids": ["20260715175719083632_2a1929df"],
"citations": [22], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260715175719083632_2a1929df", "title": "The Archaeology of Bronze Age Mycenaean 
Pylos Brewminate: A Bold Blend of News and Ideas", "url": "https://brewminate.com/the-archaeology-of-bronze-age-mycenaean-pylos", 
"source_type": "blog", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260715175720361614_faa190eb", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The earliest 
settlement, uncovered by Lord William Taylour dates to the transition between the Early and Middle Bronze Age, c. 2000 BCE.", 
"source_ids": ["20260715175719083632_2a1929df"], "citations": [22], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260715175719083632_2a1929df", 
"title": "The Archaeology of Bronze Age Mycenaean Pylos Brewminate: A Bold Blend of News and Ideas", "url": 
"https://brewminate.com/the-archaeology-of-bronze-age-mycenaean-pylos", "source_type": "blog", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": 
"20260715175720375594_86c30393", "type": "Evidence", "text": "From c. 1600 BCE, there was construction occurring on the hill of Ano 
Englianos, and by c. 1500 BCE, several monumental structures were completed. The structures on the hill burned down c. 1400 BCE, and
the new complex built there was the final Palace of Nestor.", "source_ids": ["20260715175719083632_2a1929df"], "citations": [22], 
"sources": [{"source_id": "20260715175719083632_2a1929df", "title": "The Archaeology of Bronze Age Mycenaean Pylos Brewminate: A 
Bold Blend of News and Ideas", "url": "https://brewminate.com/the-archaeology-of-bronze-age-mycenaean-pylos", "source_type": "blog",
"published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260715175720389596_65871b9d", "type": "Evidence", "text": "It consisted of a “Main Building, 
South­western Building, Northeastern Building, and Wine Magazine;” structures that stood until its final destruction c. 1180 BCE 
(Davis, 684).", "source_ids": ["20260715175719083632_2a1929df"], "citations": [22], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260715175719083632_2a1929df", "title": "The Archaeology of Bronze Age Mycenaean Pylos Brewminate: A Bold Blend of News and 
Ideas", "url": "https://brewminate.com/the-archaeology-of-bronze-age-mycenaean-pylos", "source_type": "blog", "published_at": 
null}]}, {"id": "20260715175720403725_8257097f", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Additionally, surviving decoration around the hearth 
in the megaron shows detail of flames encircling the base.", "source_ids": ["20260715175719083632_2a1929df"], "citations": [22], 
"sources": [{"source_id": "20260715175719083632_2a1929df", "title": "The Archaeology of Bronze Age Mycenaean Pylos Brewminate: A 
Bold Blend of News and Ideas", "url": "https://brewminate.com/the-archaeology-of-bronze-age-mycenaean-pylos", "source_type": "blog",
"published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260715175720417553_bd6c7a72", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Mycenaean palatial centres were 
used as centralised spaces for economic and political activities, as well as spaces for specialised crafts and redistribution of 
goods.", "source_ids": ["20260715175719083632_2a1929df"], "citations": [22], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260715175719083632_2a1929df", "title": "The Archaeology of Bronze Age Mycenaean Pylos Brewminate: A Bold Blend of News and 
Ideas", "url": "https://brewminate.com/the-archaeology-of-bronze-age-mycenaean-pylos", "source_type": "blog", "published_at": 
null}]}, {"id": "20260715175720431551_f2300ef8", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Additionally, discovered at the palace were smaller 
‘stirrup jars’, so-called for their handle which looks like a stirrup; these were used for the storage and transport of oils.", 
"source_ids": ["20260715175719083632_2a1929df"], "citations": [22], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260715175719083632_2a1929df", 
"title": "The Archaeology of Bronze Age Mycenaean Pylos Brewminate: A Bold Blend of News and Ideas", "url": 
"https://brewminate.com/the-archaeology-of-bronze-age-mycenaean-pylos", "source_type": "blog", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": 
"20260715175720445166_61df89df", "type": "Evidence", "text": "When the Palace of Nestor burned c. 1180 BCE, the clay tablets for 
their short-term administrative records were baked; preserving documents from the final year of occupation.", "source_ids": 
["20260715175719083632_2a1929df"], "citations": [22], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260715175719083632_2a1929df", "title": "The 
Archaeology of Bronze Age Mycenaean Pylos Brewminate: A Bold Blend of News and Ideas", "url": 
"https://brewminate.com/the-archaeology-of-bronze-age-mycenaean-pylos", "source_type": "blog", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": 
"20260715175720464996_bb40e461", "type": "Evidence", "text": "During excavations under Carl W. Blegen between 1952-1964 CE, William 
A. McDonald discovered two rooms containing the largest collection of Linear B tablets found in one location, which was hence 
referred to as the ‘Archive Complex.’", "source_ids": ["20260715175719083632_2a1929df"], "citations": [22], "sources": 
[{"source_id": "20260715175719083632_2a1929df", "title": "The Archaeology of Bronze Age Mycenaean Pylos Brewminate: A Bold Blend of 
News and Ideas", "url": "https://brewminate.com/the-archaeology-of-bronze-age-mycenaean-pylos", "source_type": "blog", 
"published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260715175720477917_4fe851eb", "type": "Evidence", "text": "From these well-preserved archives, 
the social stratification of Pylos and administrative functions of the Palace of Nestor were revealed.", "source_ids": 
["20260715175719083632_2a1929df"], "citations": [22], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260715175719083632_2a1929df", "title": "The 
Archaeology of Bronze Age Mycenaean Pylos Brewminate: A Bold Blend of News and Ideas", "url": 
"https://brewminate.com/the-archaeology-of-bronze-age-mycenaean-pylos", "source_type": "blog", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": 
"20260715175720490777_c48cf581", "type": "Evidence", "text": "From these archives, we know that the Mycenaean site was ruled by a 
wanax, which translates as a ‘lord’ or ‘king’. Underneath the wanax was the lawagetas, which has been understood as a governer and 
took part in economic activities and undertook military responsibilities.", "source_ids": ["20260715175719083632_2a1929df"], 
"citations": [22], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260715175719083632_2a1929df", "title": "The Archaeology of Bronze Age Mycenaean 
Pylos Brewminate: A Bold Blend of News and Ideas", "url": "https://brewminate.com/the-archaeology-of-bronze-age-mycenaean-pylos", 
"source_type": "blog", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260715175720503863_3d34d3e5", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Additionally, 
due to the Linear B tablets uncovered in the Archive Complex, we are aware that Pylos split into two areas, the Hither and Further 
provinces.", "source_ids": ["20260715175719083632_2a1929df"], "citations": [22], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260715175719083632_2a1929df", "title": "The Archaeology of Bronze Age Mycenaean Pylos Brewminate: A Bold Blend of News and 
Ideas", "url": "https://brewminate.com/the-archaeology-of-bronze-age-mycenaean-pylos", "source_type": "blog", "published_at": 
null}]}, {"id": "20260715175720517071_a1e396ca", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Surrounding the central Palace of Nestor is a rich 
mortuary landscape comprised of several chamber tombs, the so-called ‘Grave Circle’ and multiple tholoi or beehive tombs.", 
"source_ids": ["20260715175719083632_2a1929df"], "citations": [22], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260715175719083632_2a1929df", 
"title": "The Archaeology of Bronze Age Mycenaean Pylos Brewminate: A Bold Blend of News and Ideas", "url": 
"https://brewminate.com/the-archaeology-of-bronze-age-mycenaean-pylos", "source_type": "blog", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": 
"20260715175720529974_2995d8b4", "type": "Evidence", "text": "From the numerous burials located at Pylos, they are all considered 
‘elite’; the burial places for the rest of the Pylos community is unknown.", "source_ids": ["20260715175719083632_2a1929df"], 
"citations": [22], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260715175719083632_2a1929df", "title": "The Archaeology of Bronze Age Mycenaean 
Pylos Brewminate: A Bold Blend of News and Ideas", "url": "https://brewminate.com/the-archaeology-of-bronze-age-mycenaean-pylos", 
"source_type": "blog", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260715175720543127_0c604dac", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Tholos IV may 
be the oldest tholos burial on the mainland and dates to the same period as the Grave Circles at Mycenae; the Shaft Grave period.", 
"source_ids": ["20260715175719083632_2a1929df"], "citations": [22], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260715175719083632_2a1929df", 
"title": "The Archaeology of Bronze Age Mycenaean Pylos Brewminate: A Bold Blend of News and Ideas", "url": 
"https://brewminate.com/the-archaeology-of-bronze-age-mycenaean-pylos", "source_type": "blog", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": 
"20260715175720557305_d9313a36", "type": "Evidence", "text": "However, the Grave Circle at Pylos (originally considered a tholos 
tomb by Lord William Taylour), exhibits burial practices which originate from the Minoan Civilization on Crete.", "source_ids": 
["20260715175719083632_2a1929df"], "citations": [22], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260715175719083632_2a1929df", "title": "The 
Archaeology of Bronze Age Mycenaean Pylos Brewminate: A Bold Blend of News and Ideas", "url": 
"https://brewminate.com/the-archaeology-of-bronze-age-mycenaean-pylos", "source_type": "blog", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": 
"20260715175720578669_1b9e25d4", "type": "Evidence", "text": "In the Grave Circle, four large pithoi were uncovered which were used 
as burial jars.", "source_ids": ["20260715175719083632_2a1929df"], "citations": [22], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260715175719083632_2a1929df", "title": "The Archaeology of Bronze Age Mycenaean Pylos Brewminate: A Bold Blend of News and 
Ideas", "url": "https://brewminate.com/the-archaeology-of-bronze-age-mycenaean-pylos", "source_type": "blog", "published_at": 
null}]}, {"id": "20260715175720593391_1c00fa48", "type": "Evidence", "text": "This practice was not transported to the mainland 
wholesale, however, but was altered by the Pylians by choosing to bury multiple people in a single pithos; a practice not observed 
on Crete.", "source_ids": ["20260715175719083632_2a1929df"], "citations": [22], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260715175719083632_2a1929df", "title": "The Archaeology of Bronze Age Mycenaean Pylos Brewminate: A Bold Blend of News and 
Ideas", "url": "https://brewminate.com/the-archaeology-of-bronze-age-mycenaean-pylos", "source_type": "blog", "published_at": 
null}]}, {"id": "20260715175720607221_ef6af08b", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The monumental tholoi are enough to identify the 
burials in them as elite, however, the material culture discovered in all of the burials at Pylos imply great wealth and status of 
those interred.", "source_ids": ["20260715175719083632_2a1929df"], "citations": [22], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260715175719083632_2a1929df", "title": "The Archaeology of Bronze Age Mycenaean Pylos Brewminate: A Bold Blend of News and 
Ideas", "url": "https://brewminate.com/the-archaeology-of-bronze-age-mycenaean-pylos", "source_type": "blog", "published_at": 
null}]}, {"id": "20260715175720631198_963a6043", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The discoveries by Carl Blegen and his team comprised 
of gold, silver, bronze, ivory, and numerous precious and semi-precious stones. These elite materials were utilised for jewellery, 
vessels, weapons, and seal stones, in addition to numerous examples of glass paste and pottery in a range of shapes and styles.", 
"source_ids": ["20260715175719083632_2a1929df"], "citations": [22], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260715175719083632_2a1929df", 
"title": "The Archaeology of Bronze Age Mycenaean Pylos Brewminate: A Bold Blend of News and Ideas", "url": 
"https://brewminate.com/the-archaeology-of-bronze-age-mycenaean-pylos", "source_type": "blog", "published_at": null}]}], "offset": 
0, "limit": 40, "total": 56, "next_offset": 40}
