[tools:e3d2aedd-762c-9295-2be1-ba9fddf69fd1] [AIMessage → tool] commit_report_chapter({'chapter_id': 5, 'title': 'Discovery History 
and Provenance', 'content': 'The precise discovery history and provenance of the Ring of Nestor, including a securely documented 
findspot, excavation date, and museum accession number, are not fully established in the sources available here. Modern scholarship 
acknowledges that the circumstances in which the ring and certain comparable Creto–Mycenaean gold rings were found are "obscure – 
even legendary", and notes that this ambiguity contributed to earlier scholarly tendencies to regard them as forgeries.[15][17] A. 
G. Vlachopoulos explicitly describes his study as addressing erroneous identifications of such rings as fakes and as documenting the
authenticity of the so‑called Ring of Nestor (CMS VI no. 277), implying that the evidence base consists of a combination of 
documentary references, stylistic and technical analysis, and comparison with securely excavated material rather than a single, 
comprehensive excavation report.[15][17]\n\nEarly 20th‑century discussion of the ring appears in Arthur J. Evans’s publication "The 
ring of Nestor", which is presented as a study of a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and bead seals associated with sites 
such as Thisbê in Boeotia and Kakóvatos in Messenia.[18] The metadata for this work list keywords including Nestor, Minoan culture, 
and Kakóvatos, and refer to the object as part of a sepulchral treasure, suggesting that Evans linked the ring to funerary contexts 
and to a wider assemblage of rings.[18] However, the brief metadata do not specify a precise excavation trench, stratigraphic locus,
or museum accession number for the ring.[18] Later scholarship has often cited Evans’s study when referencing the ring, but modern 
authors emphasize that its early publication history and narrative framing leave room for uncertainty about the exact circumstances 
of discovery.[15][17][19]\n\nIn more recent descriptive material, an online caption refers to a "Ring of Nestor" signet ring of the 
Late Minoan period (c. 1700–1450 BC), identified as a "Minoan Kakovatos signet ring".[20] This suggests that at least some modern 
cataloguing traditions associate the ring with Kakovatos in western Messenia, a site known for rich Early Mycenaean burials with 
strong Cretan influence.[22][18] Yet the caption does not provide a detailed excavation reference, and it remains uncertain whether 
this association reflects a documented findspot or a retrospective linkage based on stylistic and regional 
considerations.\n\nTechnical studies of the ring’s authenticity and iconography further underline the limited nature of the 
provenance evidence. Ingo Pini’s article on the ring’s authenticity, as summarized in an online PDF, notes that debates have focused
on both iconography and the possibility of forgery by restorers or craftsmen such as the Gillierons, and that new technical 
observations are brought forward to support the ring’s ancient origin.[19] Vlachopoulos’s study similarly strengthens the 
authenticity case through comparison with other Late Bronze Age rings, wall‑paintings, figurines, and ivories, as well as through 
analysis of the ring’s manufacturing details.[15][17] These approaches rely on internal features of the object and external 
comparanda rather than on a detailed excavation record, and they collectively acknowledge that the ring’s discovery context is not 
documented with the same clarity as, for example, the Griffin Warrior’s tomb at Pylos, whose location, stratigraphy, and grave goods
are thoroughly published.[1][7][22]\n\nTaken together, the available sources indicate that the Ring of Nestor was part of a 
sepulchral treasure of gold rings and beads known to Evans, that later cataloguing traditions associate it with Kakovatos and Late 
Minoan or early Mycenaean contexts, and that modern scholars classify it as a Creto–Mycenaean gold signet ring of the 16th–15th 
centuries BC.[15][17][18][20] At the same time, they explicitly acknowledge that the ring’s provenance rests on fragmented 
documentary references and stylistic analysis, and that no single published excavation report currently ties the named Ring of 
Nestor to a precise findspot, discovery date, and museum accession number.[15][17][19]', 'source_ids': 
