'20260715175636967611_628ca2f8', '20260715175818162642_4e4315cf', '20260715172621170399_63f02f3a', '20260715172621254481_d57add52', 
'20260715175720347382_904f6454', '20260715173233299372_1c97e1c5', '20260715175720361614_faa190eb', '20260715174340567836_88ba374f', 
'20260715174340610572_9fee8d2c', '20260715175636821460_3233e489']}, {'title': 'Identification and Nomenclature', 'section_type': 
'body', 'content': 'The object conventionally known as the "Ring of Nestor" is identified in the Corpus der Minoischen und 
Mykenischen Siegel as CMS VI no. 277, and is consistently described in modern scholarship under this sobriquet.[15][17][18] Both A. 
G. Vlachopoulos and other recent authors refer to it as the "so‑called Ring of Nestor", indicating awareness that the name is 
traditional rather than a secure historical designation.[15][17] The CMS number links the ring to the series of Aegean sealings and 
signet rings documented in that corpus, where it is classified among large Aegean gold signet rings.[15][17]\n\nThe name "Ring of 
Nestor" was already in circulation in Arthur J. Evans’s early 20th‑century study "The ring of Nestor", which discussed a sepulchral 
treasure of gold signet rings and bead seals and linked the object to Minoan culture and sites such as Thisbê in Boeotia and 
Kakóvatos in western Peloponnese.[18] Evans’s usage helped anchor the ring in scholarly discourse and associated it loosely with the
Homeric figure Nestor, though the ring itself predates the Homeric epics and no primary textual source connects the artifact 
directly to the historical or legendary ruler of Pylos.[18][16] Later scholarship usually keeps the epithet while stressing its 
conventional nature and the absence of firm evidence that the ring belonged to an individual named Nestor.[15][17]\n\nModern 
descriptive metadata and image captions in online repositories also employ the "Ring of Nestor" label, sometimes adding information 
on cultural period and find context. One such caption describes it as a "Ring of Nestor" gold signet ring of the Late Minoan period,
dated approximately to 1700–1450 BC, and refers to it as a "Minoan Kakovatos signet ring".[20] This wording reflects a broader 
tendency to connect the ring with Minoan glyptic traditions and with Kakovatos in Messenia, a site known for rich Early Mycenaean 
burials displaying strong Cretan influence.[22][18] At the same time, scholars emphasize that the sobriquet and the proposed find 
association remain part of an interpretive tradition, not an established excavation record, and continue to reference the ring via 
its CMS catalog number when discussing technical and iconographic issues.[15][17][14]', 'source_ids': 
['20260715174314431086_1e91a4c4', '20260715174845023769_648c270f', '20260715174845033091_248864e5', '20260715175719060201_cfd2e8c2',
'20260715175719083632_2a1929df', '20260715174844930653_fe1e2804'], 'evidence_ids': ['20260715174315167475_6a2def09', 
'20260715174845693431_740a8bb4', '20260715174845763358_88603599', '20260715175720100995_50cc8fb8', '20260715175720333517_f65a1113', 
'20260715174845562658_e4d18ed8', '20260715174315041356_0f36b9fd', '20260715174315054472_5b66648a', 
'20260715174315068455_611ac5f8']}, {'title': 'Archaeological and Historical Context', 'section_type': 'body', 'content': 'The Ring 
of Nestor is generally situated within the broader archaeological and historical landscape of Bronze Age Messenia and the Palace of 
Nestor at Pylos, although no single excavation report in the available sources ties the ring unambiguously to a specific grave or 
stratigraphic context.[15][17][19] Pylos, located on the hill of Ano Englianos in southwestern Greece, was a major Mycenaean 
palatial centre between roughly 1600 and 1200 BC, covering an area of about 20–30 hectares.[22] Archaeological work has shown that 
monumental structures were constructed on the hill by around 1500 BC, and that a final complex known as the Palace of Nestor arose 
after earlier buildings burned around 1400 BC; this palace was destroyed by fire around 1180 BC, at which point its clay 
administrative tablets were baked and preserved.[22] Excavations in the 1950s and 1960s revealed an archive complex containing the 
largest collection of Linear B tablets discovered in a single location, providing detailed evidence for social stratification, 
economic administration, and the territorial division of Pylos into Hither and Further provinces ruled by a wanax (king) and 
supported by officials such as the lawagetas.[22]\n\nThe Palace of Nestor is described as the best-preserved centre of Mycenaean 
civilization and is surrounded by a rich mortuary landscape of chamber tombs, a grave circle, and multiple tholos (beehive) 
tombs.[10][22] All excavated burials in the immediate vicinity of the palace are characterized as elite, with material culture 
attesting to considerable wealth.[22] Tholos IV, near the palace, is regarded as one of the oldest tholos tombs on the mainland and 
dates to the Shaft Grave period, contemporary with grave circles at Mycenae.[22] The grave circle at Pylos shows funerary practices 
with clear Minoan antecedents, such as the use of large pithoi as burial jars, adapted locally by interring multiple individuals in 
a single jar—a practice not observed on Crete.[22] Across these contexts, grave assemblages contain gold, silver, bronze, ivory, and
numerous precious stones, used for jewellery, vessels, weapons, and seal stones, highlighting the social prominence of signet rings 
and seals in Mycenaean elite display and ritual.[22][1]\n\nWithin this landscape, the discovery in 2015 of the Griffin Warrior’s 
tomb near Tholos Tomb IV has renewed interest in early Mycenaean funerary practice and its relationships with Minoan 
culture.[1][7][8] The University of Cincinnati team uncovered a large stone-built shaft grave of Late Helladic IIA date close to the
Palace of Nestor, containing the undisturbed burial of a single adult male, aged about 30–35 years, accompanied by hundreds of 
artifacts of gold, silver, bronze, ivory, and semiprecious stones.[1][7][8] Many of these objects were manufactured in the Minoan 
world, and the grave included four gold signet rings bearing Minoan ritual imagery such as bull‑leaping scenes.[1][7][9] The 
assemblage has been interpreted as evidence for complex belief systems that intertwined Minoan ritual traditions with emerging 
Mycenaean customs and for the role of imported or hybrid iconography in the construction of elite identities and religious 
practice.[1][10][22]\n\nAlthough the Ring of Nestor itself is not explicitly listed among the grave goods of the Griffin Warrior or 
of a specific excavated tomb at Pylos in the available sources, scholars discussing the ring place it within this broader milieu of 
Creto–Mycenaean interaction, elite burial, and palatial society. Its classification as a large Aegean gold signet ring and the 
characterization of its iconography as comparable to other Late Bronze Age glyptic art suggest that it belonged to networks of 
ritual objects and personal insignia that circulated among Mycenaean elites in regions such as Messenia, perhaps including sites 
like Kakovatos and Pylos where strong Cretan influence is documented in architecture and grave goods.[15][17][18][22]', 
