'20260715172621170399_63f02f3a', '20260715172621254481_d57add52', '20260715172652702248_97e2a8da', '20260715173415706678_5c559b4b', 
'20260715173415890749_fe11be8a', '20260715175818130607_4e4315cf']}, {'title': 'Physical Description and Iconography', 
'section_type': 'body', 'content': 'The Ring of Nestor is described in modern iconographic studies as a large gold signet ring of 
the Aegean Late Bronze Age, measuring approximately 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm and ranking as the fifth largest Aegean signet ring in at 
least one comparative dataset.[15][17] Its material is gold, and stylistic and manufacturing details have been argued to align with 
other Creto–Mycenaean artifacts, supporting its attribution to the 16th–15th centuries BC.[15][17][19] Technical analyses emphasize 
the ring’s fine craftsmanship and the complexity of its engraved surface, placing it within a group of unique signet rings from the 
first centuries of the Late Bronze Age whose workmanship has been the subject of detailed study.[15][17][19]\n\nThe engraved scene 
on the ring’s bezel is characterized by scholars as one of the most populous and intricate compositions in Aegean glyptic art, 
organized around an old "tree of the world" whose branches host numerous figures and motifs.[15][17] Arthur Evans originally 
described a central zoomorphic figure as a "watch‑dog" or "dragon" guarding this world tree.[15][17][18] In more recent 
interpretations, this guardian motif is re‑identified as a scorpion, which is proposed to be a major apotropaic (protective) symbol 
in Bronze Age Aegean iconography.[15][17] Within this reading, the scorpion is said to "kill" the so‑called "Minoan dragon" on the 
ring, providing a narrative framework in which the scorpion’s protective function structures the overall scene.[15][17] The 
composition as a whole is understood as a ritual or religious tableau, though the available summaries do not list all individual 
figures, animals, or implements in detail, and some elements remain the subject of interpretive debate.[14][15][11]\n\nThe scene has
also been related to broader ritual themes by analogy with other contemporary signet rings and glyptic imagery. A news report on a 
gold signet ring discovered near the Palace of Nestor at Pylos—described as Mycenaean and bearing a detailed scene of ritual 
sacrifice with extremely fine, possibly microscopic engraving—illustrates the level of craftsmanship and ritual focus typical of 
high‑status gold rings in this region.[11] Similarly, discussions of other large Aegean signet rings, such as CMS I 179 from the 
Tiryns treasure, underline the capacity of such rings to carry complex symbolic programs involving deities, fertility motifs, and 
cosmological elements like sun and moon.[13] While these other rings differ in specific content, their combination of large size, 
gold material, and dense imagery provides a comparative framework for understanding the Ring of Nestor as part of an elite 
repertoire of ritual signet rings.\n\nIn addition to the central guardian and world tree, iconographic analyses of the Ring of 
Nestor emphasize the scene’s place within early Late Bronze Age visual traditions. Vlachopoulos notes that recent finds of 
wall‑paintings, gold rings, figurines, and ivories with comparable iconographic subjects have reinforced the view that the ring’s 
imagery belongs to an authentic Late Bronze Age repertoire rather than a modern invention.[15][17] Eliopoulos’s study of the ring’s 
iconography similarly revisits key features and comparisons, aiming to situate its motifs within Minoan and Mycenaean art and to 
contribute to ongoing debates over the ring’s significance.[14] Together, these studies portray the Ring of Nestor as a large gold 
signet ring with a densely populated ritual scene centred on a world tree and a protective scorpion motif, executed with high 
technical skill and integrated into broader Aegean iconographic traditions of the 16th–15th centuries BC.[15][17][14][19]', 
'source_ids': ['20260715174314431086_1e91a4c4', '20260715174845023769_648c270f', '20260715175636168110_c46a0b7d', 
'20260715174314422404_df622ece', '20260715174314405964_9d852ceb', '20260715174023619226_6420d547'], 'evidence_ids': 
